# Kessler — The assets this build produces

Kessler ships with no pre-made art and no pre-made sound. This machine carries
asset tools on its `PATH`, and every sprite, sheet, particle system, cue, and
music bed the game shows or plays is produced with them during this build,
committed to the repository, and bundled by it. This file is the contract: what
to produce, which tool produces it, where the file lands, and how the build
reaches it.

Production is a one-time step. The tools belong to this machine and are absent
when the project is installed and rebuilt elsewhere, so the committed files are
the assets: `npm ci` and `npm run build` invoke no tool, and the built site
fetches nothing from outside its own `dist/`. A build that shells out to a
generation tool fails wherever the tools are absent, even though the game is
complete.

## The tools

| Tool | Produces | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `draw` | one sprite, a PNG | the planet and the five salvage pod sprites |
| `draw-sheet` | a sprite sheet, one PNG per frame | the ball's spin |
| `particle-2d` | a particle system, a `system.json` | the destruction burst, the impact spark, the burn-up |
| `sfx-synth` | a procedural sound, a `.wav` | the cues |
| `sfx-sample` | a sampled sound over a baked pack, a `.wav` | the cues, where a sampled layer suits one better |
| `music` | sequenced music over a baked instrument bank, a `.wav` and a `.mid` | the two music beds |

Each is a command-line tool, and `<tool> --help` states its operations
(`<tool> <operation> --help` for one operation's flags). Each records the
operations run against it and then renders the finished file, which you commit
at the path this file names. Produce these files with these tools; pre-made
art, a downloaded sound, or a bundled font standing in for a produced file does
not meet this contract.

## The look

The mood is cold orbital demolition: hard vacuum, glass, and sunlight on dead
metal, with the planet below the one warm thing in view. Palette, type, and
styling are yours, chosen once and held across every sprite, effect, screen,
and sound so the game reads as one place.

## Scale

The 1000 x 1000 logical stage fits the viewport's short side, so at the
reference 1000-pixel fit one logical unit covers one screen pixel. Every sprite
is authored at the canvas size its table row states and drawn at that size in
logical units, centered on the object it depicts, so nothing is scaled at draw
time.

## Where the files land, and how they are loaded

Every produced file sits under `assets/` at the root of this repository, at the
path named below, and is committed.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build loads them itself. Every URL it requests resolves against the page
rather than against the origin root, so the site runs unchanged whether it is
served from the root of a static host or mounted under a sub-path. A
root-absolute URL such as `/assets/sprites/planet.png` does not meet this: it
resolves against the origin and fails under a sub-path.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine provides the asset loader and the audio cue bus,
both documented in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository. The
loader resolves every path under one root, `assets/`, relative to the page the
build is served from, so the build asks it for a path written relative to that
root, such as `sprites/planet.png` and `audio/paddle-bounce.wav`, rather than
constructing a URL of its own. Each audio cue below is bound to its produced
file through the cue bus, under its name in `CUES`.
{{/if}}

A load that fails leaves the game running. The game still initializes, still
ticks, still takes input, and still draws a legible field when a sprite, a
system, or a sound is unavailable, so a missing file costs the game its polish
rather than its playability.

## The sprites

Produce each of these on a transparent, straight-alpha canvas, and draw it at
native size, centered on its object.

| Sprite | Files | Tool | Canvas |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Planet | `assets/sprites/planet.png` | `draw` | 160 x 160 |
| Salvage pods | `assets/sprites/pods/widen.png`, `narrow.png`, `multiball.png`, `shield.png`, `pierce.png` | `draw` | 24 x 24 each |
| Ball spin | `assets/sprites/ball/0.png` to `5.png` | `draw-sheet` | 24 x 24 per frame |

The planet sprite is drawn centered on the stage center, its disc 140 pixels
across on the 160-pixel canvas, so the disc fills the planet's footprint and
the margin is free for atmosphere or glow. Each pod sprite is drawn on its
falling pod, and the HUD may reuse the pod sprites as its active-effect
indicators. The five pod kinds are told apart from each other at 24 pixels, in
flight, without reading a label.

### The ball sheet

Six frames, one PNG per frame, produced with `draw-sheet` and emitted as
separate files rather than as regions of one image. Every ball, the parked
ball included, is drawn from the sheet: the frames play `0` through `5` in
order and wrap, advancing one frame per 5 ticks of simulation time, with each
ball's phase counted from its own spawn tick. The spin runs on simulation
time.

### The art bar

- The planet reads as a planet at a glance rather than as a flat disc, and its
  disc fills the 140-pixel footprint.
- The five pod sprites are pairwise distinguishable while falling, so a player
  decides whether to catch one from its look alone.
- The six ball frames read as one continuous spin rather than a flicker, and
  the ball separates from the field at any position on the stage.
- Every sprite reads on the dark field, and none of them relies on a background
  behind it.

## The particle effects

Produce each of these with `particle-2d` as a `system.json`, and play it live.

| System | File | Fired at |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Destruction burst | `assets/particles/burst.json` | the arc center of a destroyed target |
| Impact spark | `assets/particles/spark.json` | the contact point of a paddle, containment, or shield reflection |
| Burn-up | `assets/particles/burnup.json` | a ball or pod reaching the planet |

Each system is authored radially symmetric, so an instance reads correctly at
any angle around the planet; placing and scaling an instance at its event's
position is the build's code. Each play of a system varies, and that variation
is correct.

Play them through `@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`, an installed dependency
imported by its bare name, using its `./canvas` binding: a player is
constructed over a parsed system and a 2D rendering context and advanced each
frame with that frame's delta, and it simulates the system and composites the
particles itself. Its options carry the seed, the composite mode, and whether
the context is cleared before compositing, and the package's own types are the
authoritative API.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build hands the player the same context it draws the field into, so an
effect sits over the frame at the position of the event that raised it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The context is the one `render` receives, so a live effect advances on the
frame delta `update` was handed and sits over the frame at the position of the
event that raised it.
{{else}}
A draw component is what puts a player's output on the field, so an effect
takes its place in the layer order like any other picture, at the position of
the event that raised it.
{{/if}}

## The sound

Produce a distinct sound for each of the thirteen cues, under exactly these
file names. The event each cue plays on is fixed in the file that specifies
the event.

| Cue | File | Tool |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `paddle-bounce` | `assets/audio/paddle-bounce.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `field-bounce` | `assets/audio/field-bounce.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `target-hit` | `assets/audio/target-hit.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `target-break` | `assets/audio/target-break.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `shield-reflect` | `assets/audio/shield-reflect.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `pod-catch` | `assets/audio/pod-catch.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `pod-catch-narrow` | `assets/audio/pod-catch-narrow.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `pod-burn` | `assets/audio/pod-burn.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `ball-lost` | `assets/audio/ball-lost.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `wave-clear` | `assets/audio/wave-clear.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `game-over` | `assets/audio/game-over.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `menu-move` | `assets/audio/menu-move.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `menu-select` | `assets/audio/menu-select.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |

`sfx-synth` builds a sound from synth voices alone, and `sfx-sample` layers
over a baked sample pack, which its own operations browse. Use whichever suits
each cue.

### The music beds

Produce two beds with `music`, which may sequence the baked instrument bank,
synth waveforms, or both. `music` writes a `.wav` and a `.mid` beside it; the
`.wav` is what the game plays, and keeping the `.mid` is optional.

| Bed | File | Loops on |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title bed | `assets/audio/music-title.wav` | `title` and `howto` |
| Play bed | `assets/audio/music-play.wav` | `playing`, `waveclear`, and `paused` |

The game-over screen plays no bed, so the `game-over` cue rings out over
silence. Confirming QUIT stops the play bed and the title bed resumes. Each
bed runs at least 12 seconds and loops without an audible seam, the file's end
running into its start with no click, no gap, and no jump in level, and each
sits under the cues rather than competing with them.

### The sound bar

- The palette is cold, glassy, and spacey, and the thirteen cues and two beds
  sound like one game.
- `target-break` is clearly distinct from `target-hit`, so a destruction is
  heard as a destruction on a busy field.
- `pod-catch-narrow` is told from `pod-catch` by ear alone, so a bad catch is
  heard as bad news.
- `game-over` is the heaviest sound in the game, clearly longer and lower than
  every other cue, and unmistakable as the end of a session.
- `menu-move` and `menu-select` are short and light, so working a menu never
  drowns a bed.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Decode each `.wav` with the Web Audio API, play a cue on its event, and run the
beds through looping sources.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Bind each name in `CUES` to its file with `api.audio.load`, play a cue on its
event, and run the beds through `api.audio.loop` and `api.audio.stop`.
{{else}}
Bind each name in `CUES` to its file through the engine's `audio.load`, play a
cue on its event, and run the beds through the world's `audio.loop` and
`audio.stop`.
{{/if}}

## What stays drawn in code

No produced file covers the following. Each is rendered by the build, and the
file named beside it fixes its behavior and what a player must read.

| Drawn in code | Fixed by |
| --- | --- |
| The starfield background and the containment field | `specs/field.md` |
| The three rings and their targets, including the damaged state | `specs/rings.md` |
| The deflector | `specs/deflector-and-ball.md` |
| The shield ring | `specs/pods.md` |
| The HUD, every screen, menu, and all text | `specs/screens.md` |
| The debug overlay | `specs/instrumentation.md` |

## Genuinely produced

The planet, every pod, and every ball frame on screen is a produced sprite;
every burst, spark, and burn-up is a simulated particle system; and every cue
and both beds are produced sound. Everything the game shows and plays traces to
a file produced here or to the chrome drawn in code above.
